<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Islah (Yemen)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1990–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Yemeni Congregation for Reform</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Islah, the Yemeni Congregation for Reform, is the main Islamist party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, founded in 1990 after unification. It joined Muslim Brotherhood activists with tribal leaders, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashid">Hashid</a> sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-ibn-husayn-al-ahmar">Abdullah ibn Husayn al-Ahmar</a>. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a> in the 1994 war, it later moved into opposition and backed the 2011 uprising.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-islah-yemen.jpg" alt="Al-Islah (Yemen)" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrPenguin20 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Yemeni_Congregation_for_Reform_(al-Islah).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
